# Chapter Eight: Blind Spots and Boiling Point

The cool evening air of the estate brushed against Ira’s flushed skin as she walked side-by-side with KK through the shadowed stone pathways leading back to the mansion. The lingering warmth of Shiv’s kiss still hummed in her veins, but the quiet stillness of the night could not entirely mask the heavy tension settling over the property.

Ira glanced sideways at KK, her mind racing with a mix of awe and lingering confusion. She cleared her throat softly, breaking the steady rhythm of their footsteps.

"KK..." she started hesitantly, her voice barely carrying above the rustle of the pine trees. "You've worked with him for a long time, haven't you? How is it that... under all that armor, he even functions?"

KK kept his gaze fixed straight ahead, his hands folded neatly behind his back. There was no judgment in his posture, only the quiet, unwavering loyalty of a man who had seen the worst of the world beside his boss.

"You're talking about a man who carries the weight of a multi-state empire and a family targeted by people like Bagga," KK replied evenly, his voice steady. "Shiv doesn't open up to people, Miss Ira. Most people see the cold, unyielding exterior—the walls he builds to keep everyone out. But walls work both ways. They keep threats out, yes... but they also keep him completely isolated." He paused, glancing down at her with a rare, assessing look. "Until today, I’ve never seen him tear those walls down for anyone. Not a single crack. So if you're asking me how he functions? He does it by putting everyone else's survival before his own. And right now... that includes you."

Ira’s breath hitched, the profound weight of KK's words settling deep into her chest. She wanted to ask more—to probe deeper into the dark history of the Kashyaps and the ghost of Lata—but KK expertly stepped back behind his professional boundary, offering a polite, unyielding nod as they reached the grand double doors of the mansion.

"We're here, Miss Ira. Head straight inside the secure wing," KK instructed gently, though his eyes suddenly darted toward the distant ridge of the valley. "I have to report back to Sir immediately."

---

Down in the subterranean security command center, the atmosphere was a ticking time bomb.

KK pushed the heavy steel door open to find Shiv standing motionless before a towering wall of monitors. The room was bathed in the harsh, blue glare of digital displays showing every inch of the estate. But on the far-right screen, sector four—the rugged valley perimeter facing the dense western woods—was nothing but a wall of static.

"Status," Shiv commanded, his voice dropping into a register so chillingly quiet it made the two senior security heads freeze in their tracks.

"Sir, we lost the feed for sector four about three minutes ago," the lead technician reported, his fingers flying frantically across his keyboard. "It’s not a hardware failure or a power drop. The entire node has been systematically scrubbed. Someone bypassed our local firewall."

A suffocating silence fell over the command center. Shiv didn't move a muscle, but the sheer, volcanic rage radiating from him was palpable. A single vein throbbed violently at his temple. The realization hit them all with brutal clarity: they hadn't just lost a camera. Someone was already inside the blind spot.

KK stepped forward, his eyes narrowing as he scanned the telemetry data. "It's a localized jammer, Sir. Wired directly into the ridge. It means they've been studying our routing patterns for days."

Shiv’s jaw clenched so hard a cracking sound seemed to echo in the quiet room. All the careful, measured control he had maintained all day shattered into dust. The beast he kept locked away behind his cold gray eyes broke free.

"They think they can walk into my home?" Shiv muttered, his voice a lethal, vibrating hiss. He slammed his bare palm down onto the steel console with a force that rattled the equipment, making every guard in the room flinch. "They think they can blind me and reach my family?"

Before the echoes of the impact could fade, Shiv straightened up, his mind snapping into ruthless, tactical execution. There was no hesitation left—only pure, unadulterated war.

"Listen to me carefully," Shiv ordered, his gaze sweeping over his security heads with absolute dominance. "We aren't waiting to see how many of them crossed the ridge. The mansion's perimeter is compromised."

He turned sharply to his most trusted aide. "KK. Take an advance team right now. Secure the old hunting cabin deep in the core of the upper woods. Clear the perimeter completely, check every inch, and confirm it's secure. The second it's clear, report back to me."

"Understood, Sir," KK said, already moving toward the exit. "And the family?"

"I’ll handle them," Shiv said, his jaw set in cold stone. "I'll brief them and get them ready. Once you confirm the cabin is locked down, I’ll move everyone out through the subterranean transit tunnel."

---

Ten minutes later, the heavy oak doors of the grand family lounge swung open.

Shiv walked in, instantly smoothing the iron-hard lines of his face into an easy, relaxed expression. He forced a casual, breezy smile onto his lips—a masterclass in deception designed to keep panic at bay.

Mr. and Mrs. Kashyap were seated near the fireplace, while Sindhura and Mandhira were sprawled across the sofa arguing loudly over a playlist. Ira stood near the bookshelf, her heart skipping a beat the moment she saw Shiv enter, reading the dangerous tension hidden just beneath his relaxed posture.

"Alright, pack up your bags, everyone," Shiv announced, clapping his hands together with a totally fabricated sense of enthusiasm. "Spontaneous weekend getaway."

Mandhira blinked, looking up from her phone in sheer disbelief. "A getaway? Shiv, are you feeling feverish? You practically live in your office."

"It's called spontaneity, Mandhira, try it sometime," Shiv replied smoothly, walking further into the room. "The weather up near the old northern ridge is clearing up, and I figured it’s high time we utilize the family hunting cabin. Fresh mountain air, total privacy, old-school camp vibe. Pack light, we’re heading out within the hour."

Mrs. Kashyap raised a skeptical eyebrow over her reading glasses, studying her son. "Out of nowhere? Didn't you just double the security guards downstairs five minutes ago?"

"Just routine perimeter rotation, Ma," Shiv lied effortlessly, stepping closer to her chair and offering a reassuring, warm grin. "Nothing to worry about. But I want a quiet weekend with my favorite people away from the corporate noise. Come on, think of it as a mini-vacation."

Sindhura groaned dramatically, though a grin broke across her face. "Fine, but if there's no Wi-Fi, I am holding you personally responsible for my mental breakdown, Bhai."

Shiv chuckled, though his eyes drifted across the room, landing softly on Ira, who stood silently, watching him with wide, knowing eyes. She could see right through the performance—she knew the forced smile, the casual posture, the terrifying reality of what was actually happening beneath the surface.

Shiv met her gaze, giving her a microscopic, imperceptible nod that silently commanded: *Trust me.*

"Go pack, Ira," Shiv said smoothly, turning his attention to her so naturally that no one else suspected a thing. "You're coming with us. Consider it the full Manali mountain experience."

Ira swallowed hard, the chill of true danger creeping up her spine, but she held her ground and gave a small, steady nod. The game had changed, and the trap was closing in.
